We show that lead-lag conclusions in iterative model collapse are fragile: method choice alone inflates spurious-signal rates to at least 62.5\% (inter-method $\kappa{=}0.091$--$0.185$), approaching 100\% in low-contamination regimes ($\alpha{\leq}0.50$).
We identify three pitfalls (method sensitivity, spurious regression under shared trends, and regime dependence) and propose a multi-method consensus framework requiring ${\geq}4/5$ cross-method consensus, achieving FPR${=}$0\% (0/24 pairs, three null-control seeds) and FNR${=}$0\% on effect-size reference pairs.
First-differencing cleanly separates genuine from spurious channels: all canary$\to$diversity leads survive while all canary$\to$perplexity leads vanish.
Applied to Pythia-410M and replicated on GPT-2-medium, the framework identifies token entropy as a one-generation-ahead canary for diversity collapse at full synthetic replacement (24/24 forward, 0/24 reverse, four methods, four seeds), with strong directional asymmetry (token entropy: 0\% reverse, ECE: marginal) extending to $\alpha{=}0.75$ (19/24 forward, 0/24 reverse, three seeds) in a transitional consensus regime.
